@namespace DotNetNuke.Modules.Admin.Security
@inherits DotNetNuke.Entities.Modules.PortalModuleBase

@using System
@using System.IO
@using System.Net
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using DotNetNuke.Common.Extensions
@using DotNetNuke.Data
@using DotNetNuke.Services.Localization
@using DotNetNuke.UI.Skins.Controls

@*
    Replaces accessdenied.ascx + AccessDenied.ascx.cs + AccessDenied.ascx.designer.cs.

    THIS IS THE SOLUTION-WIDE 403 SURFACE. It is reached through
    Globals.AccessDeniedURL() / AccessDeniedURL(message), which build
    NavigateURL(currentTabId, "Access Denied") - i.e. the ctl key is the ModuleControls ControlKey
    "Access Denied" (with the space), and the stored ControlSrc is `Admin/Security/AccessDenied.ascx`
    (ModuleControlID 44 / ModuleDefID 52 in the installation scripts).
    NavigationManager.NavigateURL(string) also special-cases the literal "Access Denied" and routes
    it back through Globals.AccessDeniedURL(). NEITHER the ctl key NOR the stored ControlSrc is
    changed by this conversion: the .razor is bound to the unchanged `Admin/Security/AccessDenied.ascx`
    source by admin/CoreAdminControlRegistrations.cs, so every AccessDenied redirect in the solution
    (Skin.HandleAccessDenied, Skin's tab/module permission checks, FileServerHandler, and the
    redirects the other migrated controls kept) still lands here.

    The .ascx carried NO markup at all: the whole output is the module message the code-behind adds
    through UI.Skins.Skin.AddModuleMessage, which renders into the skin's module-message
    placeholder. That is reproduced exactly, so the component itself renders nothing.

    The `message` query-string parameter is a GUID handle into the RedirectMessage table, NOT the
    message itself - a caller cannot inject display text through the URL. Both halves of the
    original guard are kept verbatim and in the same order:
    `!string.IsNullOrEmpty(guidText) && Guid.TryParse(guidText, out messageGuid)`. Only when the
    value parses as a GUID is the stored message looked up, and the looked-up text is still
    HTML-encoded (System.Net.WebUtility.HtmlEncode replaces System.Web.HttpUtility.HtmlEncode).
    The fallback remains the localized "AccessDenied" string, and the message type remains
    YellowWarning.

    The query string is read in OnLoad, which UserControlBase raises from OnParametersSet while the
    request is still being rendered server-side; the result is kept in a field, because
    HttpContext (and therefore the query string) is gone once the circuit is interactive.
*@

@code {
    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnInit(EventArgs e)
    {
        base.OnInit(e);

        // `Control.ID` is what pointed LocalResourceFile at App_LocalResources/AccessDenied.ascx;
        // there is no Control.ID any more, so the destination is set directly.
        this.LocalResourceFile = Path.Combine(this.ControlPath, Localization.LocalResourceDirectory + "/AccessDenied.ascx");
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnLoad(EventArgs e)
    {
        base.OnLoad(e);
        string message = null;
        Guid messageGuid;
        var guidText = this.Request?.GetQueryString()["message"];
        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(guidText) && Guid.TryParse(guidText, out messageGuid))
        {
            message = WebUtility.HtmlEncode(DataProvider.Instance().GetRedirectMessage(messageGuid));
        }

        DotNetNuke.UI.Skins.Skin.AddModuleMessage(
            this,
            !string.IsNullOrEmpty(message) ? message : Localization.GetString("AccessDenied", this.LocalResourceFile),
            ModuleMessage.ModuleMessageType.YellowWarning);
    }
}
